= Job's Tears (Coix, 薏苡仁)

_Scope: this entry treats the dehusked seed (caryopsis) of *Coix lacryma-jobi* L. — specifically the cultivated soft-shelled variety *var. ma-yuen* (Rom. Caill.) Stapf, whose varietal epithet commemorates the Han general Ma Yuan of the anecdote below — the Chinese drug *薏苡仁 (yìyǐrén)*, in its two classical drug-forms (生 raw and 炒 stir-fried). Status: deeply classical in Chinese medicine, *Shennong Bencao Jing* superior grade (上品), and one of the cleanest instances in this book of *藥食同源* ("medicine and food share one source") — a staple grain, a dessert ingredient, and a prescription drug with an abscess indication. Ayurveda: *thin but not absent* — the Sanskrit lexicons do carry *gavedhukā*, identified with Coix, in a wild-grain and ritual register rather than a developed dravyaguna one; the attestation is tiered honestly below rather than either denied or inflated. Tibb: absent; the modern Arabic name *damʿat Ayyūb* is a calque of the European botanical name and must not be read as Prophetic-medical pedigree. Two disambiguations: Job's tears is *not barley* (it is frequently sold as "Chinese pearl barley"; see barley.typ), and the hard-shelled wild form used for rosary beads is not the food/drug variety._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* *_Coix lacryma-jobi_ L.*, family Poaceae (Gramineae), tribe Andropogoneae — placing it, perhaps surprisingly, in the same tribe as *maize, sorghum, and sugarcane* rather than near wheat or barley. Two varieties matter and are routinely conflated:
- *var. _ma-yuen_ (Rom. Caill.) Stapf* — the *cultivated* soft-shelled form, thin-husked and threshable, which is the *food and medicinal* plant: Chinese 薏苡仁, Japanese hatomugi, Korean yulmu. *The epithet honours Ma Yuan* (馬援), the Han general of the pearls anecdote below — a rare case of a classical Chinese historical figure embedded in Linnaean-tradition botanical nomenclature.
- *var. _lacryma-jobi_* — the *wild* hard-shelled form, with a stony, enamel-hard, grey-to-black utricle; essentially inedible without heroic effort and used worldwide for *beads* (rosaries, necklaces, rattles). *The two are not interchangeable*, and popular sources conflating "Job's tears beads" with the food grain are describing different material.#footnote[Varietal distinction (soft-shelled cultivated var. _ma-yuen_ as the food/drug plant vs. hard-shelled wild var. _lacryma-jobi_ as the bead plant) and tribal placement in Andropogoneae: standard agrostological and crop-botany references; _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ specifies the cultivated variety for the drug 薏苡仁. The _ma-yuen_ epithet commemorates Ma Yuan — standard in the botanical-nomenclature literature on the species.]

*Genus etymology — a flagged Linnaean false friend:* *Coix* is from Greek *κόϊξ (kóïx)*, a name used by Theophrastus and other classical authors for an *Egyptian palm* (generally identified with a _Hyphaene_ doum palm), a plant with no relation whatever to this Asian grass. Linnaeus's reuse of the word is a loose recycling of an available classical name, not an etymological statement — the same class of accident as _lýkion_/_Lycium_ in `goji-berry.typ` and _Chrysanthemum_ in `chrysanthemum.typ`.#footnote[Theophrastus, _Historia Plantarum_, for the Greek _kóïx_ (an Egyptian palm, usually identified as _Hyphaene_); Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. κόϊξ). Linnaeus's transfer of the name to this grass is documented in the botanical-nomenclature literature and carries no implication of classical attestation for the Asian plant.]

*Species epithet and English name:* *lacryma-jobi*, Latin *"tear of Job"* — for the hard, oval, polished, tear-shaped utricle of the wild form, strung as beads across many cultures; named for *Job*, the Biblical archetype of suffering and weeping (Book of Job). *English* *"Job's tears"* is a direct calque. Also *adlay* (from Philippine usage, for the cultivated grain), *coix seed*, and — *flagged as misleading* — *"Chinese pearl barley"* or simply "pearl barley" in some Asian groceries: Job's tears is *not* _Hordeum vulgare_ and is not botanically close to it; the two are confusable in appearance when polished, differ in protein, fat, and texture, and *only barley contains gluten-related prolamins* (see Modern Pharmacology, and `barley.typ`).

*Chinese:* *薏苡 (yìyǐ)* the plant; *薏苡仁 (yìyǐrén)* the dehusked seed — the official drug name; colloquially *薏仁 / 薏米 (yìrén / yìmǐ)*, the form in which it appears in Taiwanese and Hong Kong dessert and soup culture. The drug is in the *神農本草經 (Shennong Bencao Jing)*, *上品 (superior grade)*. *Premium origins:* 貴州興仁 (Xingren, Guizhou) and 福建浦城 (Pucheng, Fujian) are the principal recognized production zones in modern commerce.

*The pearls anecdote — 薏苡明珠 (yìyǐ míngzhū).* The Eastern Han general *馬援 (Ma Yuan, 14 BCE–49 CE)*, campaigning in the far south (Jiaozhi, in the region of modern Guangxi and northern Vietnam), found that yìyǐrén relieved the damp-miasmal illness (*瘴氣, zhàngqì*) afflicting his troops in that hot wet climate, and on returning north brought back cartloads of the seed. After his death, rivals slandered him to the throne, alleging that the carts had carried *looted pearls and treasure* — a charge grave enough to disgrace the Ma family until it was formally cleared later. The resulting idiom *薏苡明珠* ("Job's tears [taken for] bright pearls") still means *to be falsely accused through malicious misconstruction of an innocent act*. The episode is in Ma Yuan's biography in the *後漢書 (Hòu Hàn Shū)*, compiled by Fan Ye in the 5th c. *It is also the earliest clear record of the drug's climatic-medical use* — a damp-draining grain carried north from a hot-humid campaign — and it is commemorated in the plant's botanical varietal name.#footnote[_Hòu Hàn Shū_ (後漢書), Fan Ye, 5th c., 馬援列傳 (Biography of Ma Yuan) — the standard classical source; 薏苡明珠 is a standard chengyu derived from it. The anecdote's medical content (yìyǐrén against southern 瘴氣 damp-miasmal illness) is an early attestation of the 利水滲濕 indication in a specific climatic setting, and is picked up again in the Climate section below.]

*Classical drug-forms:*
- *生薏苡仁 (shēng yìyǐrén, raw)* — the stronger *damp-draining, heat-clearing, and pus-expelling* form; the form used in abscess and damp-Bì formulas.
- *炒薏苡仁 / 麩炒薏苡仁 (chǎo / fū-chǎo yìyǐrén, dry-fried or bran-fried)* — the *Spleen-strengthening and diarrhoea-binding* form; frying moderates the cool draining character and brings forward the grain's tonifying, astringent-binding side. The same raw/processed logic as 生薑/乾薑 ginger and 生地/熟地 rehmannia (see `ginger.typ`, `rehmannia.typ`), and the choice between forms is clinically load-bearing, not cosmetic.

*Japanese:* *鳩麦 / ハトムギ (hatomugi, "pigeon wheat")* — the grain and the ubiquitous roasted *hatomugi-cha*; and *ヨクイニン (yokuinin)*, the Sino-Japanese reading of 薏苡仁, which in Japan names a *specific and widely used over-the-counter indication*: yokuinin preparations are sold and prescribed for *warts (verruca vulgaris)*, an application developed in modern Japanese practice out of the classical 解毒散結 register (see Modern Pharmacology). Hatomugi extract is also a major ingredient in Japanese and Korean skincare.

*Korean:* *율무 (yulmu)*; *율무차 (yulmu-cha)*, a thick sweetened Job's-tears drink, is a standard Korean traditional beverage.

*Sanskrit — thin but real, and tiered honestly:* the lexicons do carry *गवेधुका / गवेधु (gavedhukā / gavedhu)*, glossed by Monier-Williams as a kind of grass and identified with *Coix* (MW gives "Coix barbata," an older name in the same genus). The name appears in the *Vedic and ritual* literature as a wild grass-grain and in the *lexicographic* tradition, rather than in a developed dravyaguna treatment with assigned rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka. *What this supports and what it does not:* it supports the claim that the plant was known and named in Sanskrit (so a flat "absent from India" would be wrong); it does *not* establish a core-saṃhitā medicinal monograph. The identification of _gavedhukā_ should be verified against a specific Bhāvaprakāśa or Rāja Nighaṇṭu edition. Modern regional and tribal use in the Northeast Indian hills, Myanmar, and the Southeast Asian uplands is documented ethnobotanically and is *(F)-tier* folk/regional.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit-English Dictionary_ (s.v. गवेधु, गवेधुका — a grass, identified with _Coix barbata_); the name's occurrence in Vedic/ritual and lexicographic rather than developed dravyaguna contexts. Whether the core saṃhitās (Caraka, Suśruta) or the major nighaṇṭus assign dravyaguna parameters to _gavedhukā_ is *not* established here and is flagged for verification against specific editions. Northeast Indian, Myanmar, and upland Southeast Asian ethnobotanical use: regional ethnobotanical survey literature — (F)-tier.]

*Arabic / Persian — a naming trap, flagged:* modern Arabic *دمعة أيوب (damʿat Ayyūb, "Job's tear")* is a *direct calque of the European botanical name*, coined in modern scientific-Arabic nomenclature; Persian likewise uses translated/transliterated forms. *This must not be read as attestation.* The plant has *no* entry in Avicenna or Ibn al-Bayṭār, and although *Ayyūb (Job)* is a Qur'ānic prophet (Qur'ān 21:83–84; 38:41–44), the plant's name invokes him only through a 17th–18th-c. European botanical metaphor — *there is no Ṭibb al-Nabawī standing whatever*, and the apparent scriptural resonance of the Arabic name is an artefact of translation. See the Tibb section.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

薏苡仁 is *deeply classical* — a _Shennong Bencao Jing_ 上品 drug, present in Zhang Zhongjing's formulary, and simultaneously an ordinary grain. It is the standard teaching example, alongside 山藥 and 蓮子, of *藥食同源 (yào shí tóng yuán)*: the same substance is a prescription drug at 30 g in decoction and a dessert at a bowlful.

*Property (性 xìng):* Cool (涼), given as *slightly cold (微寒)* in many sources; the *stir-fried* form is nearer neutral.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘), *Bland (淡)* — and as with fúlíng, the *bland* flavour is the operative one, under the classical principle *淡味滲泄* ("the bland percolates and discharges"): blandness is a positive drainage category, not an absence of taste. See `fuling.typ`, where the same principle and its lack of an Ayurvedic counterpart are discussed.

*Channels (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺). The *Lung* entry is what distinguishes it from fúlíng and underwrites its signature abscess indication.

*Actions and indications:*
- *利水滲濕 (lì shuǐ shèn shī)* — promotes urination and leaches out dampness: edema, damp-type diarrhoea, turbid urine, damp accumulation generally. The classic pairing is with *茯苓* (see `fuling.typ`), the two being the canonical bland-sweet damp-drainers; yìyǐrén appears with it in *參苓白朮散 (Shēn Líng Bái Zhú Sǎn)*, the Spleen-qi-tonifying formula built on 四君子湯.
- *健脾止瀉 (jiàn pí zhǐ xiè)* — strengthens the Spleen and checks diarrhoea: principally the *stir-fried* form, for Spleen-vacuity loose stools — the one indication for which the processed form is clearly preferred.
- *除濕除痺 (chú shī chú bì)* — dispels damp and relieves *Bì* obstruction: damp-type joint and muscle pain, heaviness, swelling. Two Zhang Zhongjing formulas carry this register: *麻杏薏甘湯 (Má Xìng Yì Gān Tāng,* Ephedra, Apricot Kernel, Coix and Licorice Decoction) from the *金匱要略 (Jīn Guì Yào Lüè)*, for wind-damp body aches and afternoon fever; and *薏苡仁湯 (Yìyǐrén Tāng)* for damp-Bì of the limbs.
- *清熱排膿 (qīng rè pái nóng)* — clears heat and expels pus: *the drug's most distinctive classical action*, and the reason for its Lung-channel entry. The *raw* form is specified. Two canonical formulas: *葦莖湯 (Wěijīng Tāng,* Reed Stem Decoction) for *肺癰 (lung abscess)* with cough and foul purulent sputum — preserved in Sun Simiao's *千金要方 (Qiānjīn Yàofāng,* 7th c.) and conventionally assigned to the Zhang Zhongjing tradition; and *薏苡附子敗醬散 (Yìyǐ Fùzǐ Bàijiàng Sǎn)* from the *金匱要略* for *腸癰 (intestinal abscess)*, the formula traditionally read against what modern medicine would call appendiceal or pelvic abscess. *That a bland cereal grain is a principal drug for internal suppuration is genuinely counterintuitive*, and is the basis for the prabhāva-level reading proposed in the Ayurveda section.
- *Damp-warmth (濕溫) formulary:* *三仁湯 (Sān Rén Tāng,* Three-Seed Decoction), from Wu Jutang's *溫病條辨* (1798; the same source as 桑菊飲, see `chrysanthemum.typ`), pairs yìyǐrén with apricot kernel and cardamom to address damp-warmth at three levels (upper, middle, lower) — the Wen Bing school's signature approach to damp-heat, and one of the most-used damp-warmth formulas in modern practice.
- *解毒散結 (jiě dú sàn jié)* — resolves toxicity and disperses nodules: the register from which two modern applications grew — the Japanese *yokuinin* wart indication and the Chinese *康萊特 (Kanglaite)* coix-seed-oil injectable used as an adjuvant in oncology. *Both are modern developments*, flagged as such in Modern Pharmacology, not classical formulas.

*Cautions:*
- *Pregnancy — a genuine classical caution, not merely a modern flag.* Chinese materia medica attaches *妊娠慎用 ("use with caution in pregnancy")* to 薏苡仁, and modern pharmacological work has reported uterine-stimulant activity in animal preparations. This is one of the cleaner cases in this book of a classical caution *later supported* rather than contradicted by pharmacology, and it is treated as *(S/N) + (M) convergent* in the Viruddha section.
- *Constipation and dryness:* the drug is draining and, in the fried form, binding; in constipation, fluid-deficiency, or frank dryness it is inappropriate.
- *Slow action:* classical sources note that yìyǐrén is *mild and slow* and must be given in *larger doses and over time* to act — hence the 30 g upper range, unusual for a drug of such gentleness.#footnote[薏苡仁 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (上品); Zhang Zhongjing, _Jīn Guì Yào Lüè_ (金匱要略) for 麻杏薏甘湯 and 薏苡附子敗醬散; Sun Simiao, _Qiānjīn Yàofāng_ (千金要方), 7th c., for 葦莖湯; Wu Jutang, _Wēn Bìng Tiáo Biàn_ (溫病條辨), 1798, for 三仁湯; _Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ lineage for 參苓白朮散. Pregnancy caution, raw/fried form distinction, and the "mild and slow, needs larger dose" note: Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ monograph.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Mixed status, tiered rather than flattened: the_ name _is classical (*gavedhukā* in the Sanskrit lexicons, identified with Coix — see Nomenclature), the_ register _is wild-grain and ritual rather than medicinal, and no developed dravyaguna monograph has been confirmed in the core saṃhitās or major nighaṇṭus. Regional and tribal food-and-medicine use in the Northeast Indian hills and the Southeast Asian uplands is (F)-tier. The reading below is therefore_ reasoned extension anchored on a genuine Sanskrit name _— a middle case between the fully classical entries of this book and the wholly reasoned ones (`sage.typ`, `fuling.typ`), and flagged as such._

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet — the grain base), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — the binding note that corresponds to the fried form's 止瀉 action), with the *bland* character of the Chinese 淡 again finding no exact rasa counterpart (see `fuling.typ` for the structural point).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light — notably so for a cereal), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry), Sūkṣma (सूक्ष्म, subtle); *Sāndra/sthira* in the cooked grain as food, which is why it nourishes as a staple while draining as a drug.

*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — converging with the Chinese 涼/微寒; the stir-fried form reads as *anuṣṇāśīta* (neutral), the classical rendering of processing-shifted potency.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).

*Prabhāva:* *None classically attested* (no dravyaguna monograph). If one were singled out, the candidate is strong and specific: *the expulsion of internal suppuration* — the 清熱排膿 action behind 葦莖湯 and 薏苡附子敗醬散, by which a bland, cooling, lightly astringent cereal resolves *lung and intestinal abscess*. That action is *not* derivable from madhura-kaṣāya rasa, laghu-rūkṣa guṇa, śīta vīrya, and madhura vipāka — a nourishing cool grain should not drain pus — which is exactly the prabhāva criterion as Caraka states it (Sū. 26.67–72). Flagged as *interpretive*: the reading is mine, applied to a classical Chinese indication, not a classical Ayurvedic claim.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Kapha-śāmaka (governing — laghu-rūkṣa against _kleda_ and damp); Pitta-śāmaka (śīta-madhura); *Vāta-vardhaka in excess* (laghu-rūkṣa-śīta, and the diuretic depletion of _rasa-dhātu_) — the dravyaguna statement of the Chinese dryness-and-constipation caution.

*Reasoned karma:*
- _Mūtrala_ — diuretic (利水).
- _Śothahara_ — anti-edema (消腫).
- _Grāhī_ — binding/anti-diarrhoeal, specifically of the *fried* form (炒薏苡仁 健脾止瀉).
- _Vraṇa-śodhana_ — wound- and abscess-cleansing; the dravyaguna rendering of 排膿, and the candidate prabhāva above.
- _Āmavāta-hara / sandhi-śoṣaṇa_ — relieving damp-type joint pain and heaviness (除痺) — the register in which an Ayurvedic physician would place the 麻杏薏甘湯 indication.
- _Balya, bṛṃhaṇa_ — strengthening and nourishing, in its capacity as a *food grain* (and the reason the same substance can be both _laṅghana_-adjacent as a drug and _bṛṃhaṇa_ as a staple — a genuine internal tension in the reading, noted rather than resolved).
- _Tvacya_ — skin-benefiting; the reasoned reading of the East Asian cosmetic/wart register.#footnote[Reasoned placement anchored on the genuine Sanskrit lexical name (_gavedhukā_) but rendered from the classical Chinese indication-set; it is *not* a classical Ayurvedic monograph — flagged. Prabhāva criterion: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72. The _laṅghana_/_bṛṃhaṇa_ tension between the drug use and the food use is left standing rather than smoothed, since it is the substantive content of 藥食同源 for this particular substance.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical Ayurvedic viruddha — extensions of conditional rules plus the convergent classical-Chinese and modern cautions; tags: S core saṃhitā, N nighaṇṭu, F folk/regional, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S/N) + (M) Pregnancy — the one firm contraindication, and classically-and-modernly convergent.* Chinese materia medica's *妊娠慎用* caution and modern reports of uterine-stimulant activity in animal models point the same way; *medicinal doses are to be avoided in pregnancy*, and ordinary dietary amounts in soups and desserts are the grey zone where a practitioner should be consulted rather than a rule asserted. Tagged across tiers deliberately, because the convergence is the point.
- *(extended) Conditional:* _Doṣa-viruddha_ in Vāta excess and in dry constitutions; _avasthā-viruddha_ in constipation, dehydration, and frank fluid depletion (draining plus binding is the wrong combination there); _agni-viruddha_ where _agni_ is weak and the cool heavy-when-cooked grain burdens digestion; _mātrā-viruddha_ — relevant in the unusual direction for this drug, since classical sources say it is *too weak at small dose* to act.
- *(F) Folk/regional:* the Cantonese and Taiwanese dessert-soup register (薏米水, 四神湯) treats it as an everyday food; Korean *yulmu-cha* and Japanese *hatomugi-cha* likewise. Living folk dietetics, (F)-tier, and the context of most actual consumption.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Hypoglycaemic additivity* — modest glucose-lowering signals in animal and small human studies; theoretical additive effect with antidiabetic medication at high intake. Theoretical, flagged.
  - *Not a gluten source, and a positive note for once:* Job's tears is in Andropogoneae, *not* Triticeae, and contains no gluten-related prolamins — so unlike barley (`barley.typ`, `wheat.typ`) it is suitable in coeliac disease, *provided* it has not been sold or processed as "pearl barley" alongside true barley with the attendant cross-contact risk. The naming confusion is thus a real food-safety matter for coeliac readers, not merely a pedantic one.
  - *Kanglaite (injectable coix oil)* — a pharmaceutical, not a food or herb preparation; its interactions and adverse-event profile belong to the drug-regulatory literature and are not addressed by herbal dosing.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Coix lacryma-jobi _has no entry in Avicenna's_ Qānūn _or Ibn al-Bayṭār's_ al-Jāmiʿ_: an East and Southeast Asian monsoon grass outside the reach of medieval Arabic pharmacy, which received Asian aromatics and resins by trade but not bulk cereals. A specific warning applies here that does not apply to the other reasoned-only entries in this book: the modern Arabic name دمعة أيوب (damʿat Ayyūb, "Job's tear") is a calque of the European botanical epithet, and because Ayyūb/Job is a Qur'ānic prophet, the name can be — and in popular herbal writing sometimes is — misread as Prophetic-medical pedigree. There is none. The reading below is reasoned extension on the humoral axis._ See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

*Names:* modern Arabic *دمعة أيوب (damʿat Ayyūb)* — a modern scientific calque, not a classical drug-name; no classical Persian or Urdu-Hakeemi name. Modern Unani practice in South Asia has not adopted the drug.

*Reasoned mizāj:* *Cold and Dry, 1st degree* — the humoral rendering of 涼/微寒 with the light-dry draining character; the *fried* form would read as temperate (*muʿtadil*) and drier, the processing shift being a device Tibb also recognizes (roasting as a mizāj-modifier).

*Reasoned afʿāl (actions):* *Mudirr li-l-bawl* (diuretic — governing); *mujaffif* (desiccant); *qābiḍ* (astringent — the fried form's binding action); *munaqqī li-l-ṣadr* and *mukhrij al-qayḥ* (chest-cleansing and pus-expelling — the humoral rendering of 排膿, and the action a Tibb physician would find most remarkable in a cereal); *mughadhdhī* (nourishing, in its food capacity); *muḥallil* (resolvent) for the joint-damp register.

*Reasoned istemālāt (indications):* *istisqā'* (dropsy/edema); *amrāḍ al-balgham* (phlegmatic conditions, the humoral analogue of damp accumulation); *amrāḍ al-mafāṣil* (joint diseases) of the cold-damp type; *jurūḥ wa-quruḥ* and internal suppuration — the abscess register; diarrhoea of the relaxed-bowel type (fried form).

*Reasoned mawāneʿ (contraindications):* cold-phlegmatic (*balghamī*) temperaments would in fact be the *indication* here rather than the contraindication; the contrary states are *dry (yābis)* temperaments, depletion, and constipation. Pregnancy, on the convergent evidence above.

*Reasoned muṣliḥ / badal:* a Cold-Dry draining cereal would conventionally be corrected with a warming aromatic — *dārchīnī* (cinnamon), *zanjabīl* (ginger), or *anīsūn* — exactly as the Chinese formulary pairs it with 附子 in 薏苡附子敗醬散 and with 麻黃 in 麻杏薏甘湯, a convergence of correction-logic across systems that is worth noting. Plausible *badal* within the actual Tibb pharmacopoeia: *shaʿīr* (barley) for the nourishing-demulcent-diuretic register, which is also the source of the English naming confusion. *Explicitly reasoned, not textual.*

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *none* — and the Arabic name's invocation of the prophet Ayyūb is a modern translation artefact, as flagged above. Recorded emphatically because this is the one entry in this book where a plant's modern Arabic name actively invites a false Prophetic pedigree.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *Damp-Bì and wind-damp:* *麻杏薏甘湯* (_Jīn Guì Yào Lüè_ — wind-damp body aches, afternoon fever); *薏苡仁湯* (damp-Bì of the limbs).
- *Abscess and suppuration (raw form):* *葦莖湯* (lung abscess; Sun Simiao, _Qiānjīn Yàofāng_); *薏苡附子敗醬散* (intestinal abscess; _Jīn Guì Yào Lüè_, with aconite and _Patrinia_).
- *Damp-warmth:* *三仁湯* (_Wēn Bìng Tiáo Biàn_, 1798 — with apricot kernel and cardamom, addressing damp-heat at three levels).
- *Spleen-tonifying:* *參苓白朮散* (with 茯苓, 山藥, 蓮子 — see `fuling.typ`).
- *Dietetic preparations (藥食同源 in practice):* *四神湯 (sìshén tāng,* "four spirits soup" — Job's tears with lotus seed, Chinese yam, and poria or _Euryale_ seed; a Fujianese-Taiwanese household staple, see `fuling.typ`); *薏米水 (yìmǐ shuǐ,* Job's-tears water) and *薏米綠豆湯* (with mung bean — see `mung-dhal.typ`) in Cantonese *糖水* and *涼茶* registers; *薏米粥* (congee); *薏仁湯* desserts in Taiwan and Hong Kong; Job's tears in Cantonese *老火湯* long-simmered soups.

=== Ayurveda

*No classical Ayurvedic formula* — the Sanskrit name *gavedhukā* sits in lexical and ritual, not formulary, contexts (see above), and no compound preparation built on it has been identified. Regional and tribal preparations in the Northeast Indian hills (porridges, and in some communities fermented beverages) are *(F)-tier folk* rather than classical-formulary.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

*No classical Greco-Roman or European medical tradition.* The substantive Western register for this plant is *material-cultural rather than medicinal*, and is worth recording precisely:
- *Rosary and prayer beads.* The hard-shelled wild form's seeds have been strung as beads across the Americas, the Philippines, and parts of Europe — "Job's tears" rosaries are a documented Catholic devotional-material tradition, and the seeds also serve in rattles and ornaments. *The Job association in the name is European-botanical and devotional, not pharmacological.*
- *Ornamental and famine food.* The plant is grown ornamentally in Europe and North America, and the grain has been used as a famine or curiosity food; neither amounts to a medical tradition.
- *Modern Western commerce.* Job's tears now appears in Western health-food markets as a gluten-free grain and in East-Asian-derived supplement and skincare products — post-classical and commercial.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic)

*No classical preparation* — see the Tibb section above, including the *damʿat Ayyūb* naming caveat.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Job's tears is a *hot-humid monsoon* grass of East and Southeast Asian origin, favouring moist soils, paddy margins, streambanks, and upland swidden fields. Its climatic story is unusually tidy: *the plant of the damp climate is the drug of damp pathology*, and the earliest record of its medical use — Ma Yuan's southern campaign — is explicitly a story about carrying a damp-climate remedy out of the damp climate. See `climates.typ`.

=== Hot-humid monsoon East and Southeast Asia (the native and canonical zone)

Köppen Am/Aw/Cfa/Cwa: long hot wet season, heavy rainfall, high humidity. External *濕* and *濕熱*, with *Spleen* loading per the 六淫 correspondence (濕→脾); Pitta-Kapha with weakened _agni_ in dravyaguna terms. This is the zone of the Ma Yuan anecdote (Jiaozhi/Lingnan), of the Guizhou and Fujian production areas, and of the drug's core indications. *Traditional preparation and cares.* Two constitutive registers: the *medicinal decoction* at 15–30 g, raw for draining and abscess, fried for Spleen-support and diarrhoea; and the *dietetic* one — *薏米水*, *薏米綠豆湯*, *四神湯*, congee, and Cantonese *糖水* — in which long simmering and combination with neutral or warming tonics (yam, lotus seed, jujube) build in the balance the lone drug lacks. *The cares are the combinations:* yìyǐrén alone, cold, and habitually, in a hot-damp climate would drain without supporting; the folk preparations never use it alone.

=== Temperate East Asia (Japan and Korea — the roasted-beverage and cosmetic registers)

Köppen Cfa/Dfa, with cold winters absent from the plant's native range. Here the register shifts from decoction to *roasted grain beverage* — Japanese *hatomugi-cha*, Korean *yulmu-cha* (thickened and sweetened, often drunk hot in winter) — and to *topical/cosmetic* use (hatomugi skincare). *The roasting is the climatic adaptation*: it converts a cool draining grain into a warm, nourishing, mildly astringent drink appropriate to a cold-winter zone, exactly the _saṃskāra_ logic (and the 炒 drug-form logic) by which the same substance is made climatically apt. Japan also developed the distinctive *yokuinin* wart indication, a pharmaceutical rather than dietetic register.

=== Upland South and Southeast Asia (the tribal grain zone)

The Northeast Indian hills, upland Myanmar, northern Thailand and Laos, and indigenous Taiwan: Job's tears as a *swidden-cultivated subsistence grain*, eaten as porridge and, in a number of communities, used in *traditional fermented beverages*. This is the zone where the Sanskrit *gavedhukā* register and the (F)-tier ethnobotanical record belong — a food-grain tradition rather than a pharmacological one, and the reason this book treats the Indian attestation as real but thin.#footnote[Upland swidden cultivation of _Coix_ as a subsistence grain and its use in traditional fermented beverages across Northeast India, upland Myanmar, northern mainland Southeast Asia, and among indigenous Taiwanese communities: regional ethnobotanical and agricultural-history literature — (F)-tier regional attestation; specific community practices vary and should be sourced individually rather than generalized.]

=== Climates where Job's tears is not traditional

The Mediterranean, the Middle East, Europe, Africa, and the Americas have no traditional use — the Greco-Roman and Tibb absences treated above are geographic, and the Western presence is ornamental, devotional (beads), or modern-commercial. The *indications* travel (damp patterns and edema are universal); the *tradition* does not.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_ / _kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

Three transposition issues, in descending order of practical importance. First, *identity*: sold internationally as "Chinese pearl barley," the grain is mistaken for barley — a _sampad-viruddha_ (wrong-substance) problem with a real consequence, since coeliac consumers may either avoid a safe grain or buy cross-contacted material (see Viruddha). Second, *form and season*: the cool draining decoction register has travelled into year-round use in cold-temperate climates, where the East Asian traditions themselves reached for the *roasted* form — taking the cooling preparation through a cold damp winter is the _kāla-viruddha_ case, and the Japanese and Korean roasted beverages are the tradition's own correction. Third, *framing*: the modern Western and East Asian cosmetic marketing of hatomugi for "skin whitening" detaches the substance from any pattern-based indication and rests on weak evidence (see Modern Pharmacology) — not a _viruddha_ category so much as a claim-inflation problem, but worth naming. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Job's tears is genuinely *both* food and drug, so both profiles are given.

Composition, Job's tears (coix seed), raw, approximate per 100 g — *verify against current food-composition databases*, as published values vary with variety and degree of polishing.

- Energy ~350–380 kcal; Water ~11 g
- *Protein ~12–15 g* — *notably higher than rice (~7 g) and comparable to or above wheat*; one of the grain's genuine nutritional distinctions
- *Fat ~5–6 g* — high for a cereal (cf. rice ~1 g), and the fraction from which the medicinal oil preparations are made
- Carbohydrate ~65 g; dietary fibre ~1–2 g (higher in less-polished material)
- B vitamins (thiamine, niacin); potassium, magnesium, phosphorus, iron
- *No gluten-related prolamins* — see Viruddha; relevant to coeliac disease, subject to the "pearl barley" cross-contact caveat

*Key constituents (medicinal):*
- *Coixenolide* — a characteristic triglyceride-class constituent of the seed oil, long proposed as the principal antitumour component and the rationale for the *Kanglaite* preparation.
- *Coixol* — *6-methoxybenzoxazolinone (6-MBOA)*, a benzoxazinoid with reported sedative, muscle-relaxant, and anti-inflammatory activity in animal models; characteristic of the genus.
- *Coix lactams*, benzoxazinoid derivatives, free and bound phenolics.
- *Polysaccharides* (coixans) — hypoglycaemic activity reported in animal models.
- *Adlay bran oil* — the lipid fraction carrying much of the anti-inflammatory and antiproliferative preclinical activity; concentrated in the bran, so *degree of polishing materially affects the medicinal content* — a modern point with a direct bearing on commercial material quality.#footnote[Phytochemistry: coixenolide; coixol (6-methoxybenzoxazolinone/6-MBOA) and its sedative/muscle-relaxant animal pharmacology; coix lactams; coixan polysaccharides; the bran-concentrated lipid fraction and the effect of polishing on medicinal content — standard _Coix_ phytochemistry reviews; verify against current literature.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Antitumour / Kanglaite — the most consequential modern claim, and the one needing the most careful framing.* *康萊特 (Kanglaite)*, an injectable emulsion of coix seed oil, is *approved in China* as an adjuvant in the treatment of certain cancers (notably non-small-cell lung and hepatic), and is also used in veterinary and palliative contexts. *Important qualifications:* the supporting evidence is predominantly Chinese-language trials of *heterogeneous and often limited methodological quality*; the preparation is *not approved in the United States or European Union*; and its standing is as an *adjuvant* alongside conventional therapy, not as a treatment in its own right. Any reader encountering "coix cures cancer" claims should be referred to this distinction.
- *Yokuinin for warts (Japan)* — oral yokuinin (薏苡仁) extract is a long-standing Japanese OTC and prescribed treatment for *verruca vulgaris*, with a modest clinical literature (small trials and case series, some with reasonable response rates) and a proposed immunomodulatory mechanism. *Modest evidence, genuine clinical use*; one of the better-documented modern applications of a classical Chinese drug within Japanese practice.
- *Anti-inflammatory* — the bran lipid fraction and coixol show activity in standard models; reasonably consistent preclinically, and congruent with both the 清熱排膿 and 除痺 classical registers.
- *Hypoglycaemic and lipid-lowering* — coixan polysaccharides and whole-grain feeding lower glucose and lipids in animal models, with small human studies suggesting modest effects. Preliminary.
- *Diuretic* — consistent with 利水 but, as with fúlíng, *human controlled evidence is thin*; the classical action outruns the clinical literature, and saying so is more useful than asserting the mechanism.
- *Skin / "whitening"* — the East Asian cosmetic register (hatomugi extracts) rests on *weak evidence*: in vitro tyrosinase-inhibition and small cosmetic-industry studies, with no robust clinical demonstration. Flagged as commercially driven.#footnote[Kanglaite: Chinese National Medical Products Administration approval records; systematic reviews of Kanglaite trials consistently note heterogeneous methodology and the absence of US/EU approval — verify current regulatory standing before any claim of equivalence. Yokuinin for verruca: Japanese clinical literature (small trials and case series) — modest evidence, genuine practice. Anti-inflammatory, hypoglycaemic, and diuretic findings: standard _Coix_ pharmacology reviews, preclinical-to-preliminary. Cosmetic "whitening" claims: in vitro and industry studies only — flagged as weak, per this book's standard on over-claimed effects.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* Job's tears has *no toxicological ceiling as a food* and the one firm medicinal restriction is *pregnancy*.
- *As food:* unrestricted at ordinary dietary intake — soups, congee, desserts, roasted beverages; eaten as a staple in several regions.
- *Medicinal decoction:* *9–30 g/day*, with the upper half of the range usual, since classical sources specifically note the drug is *too weak at small dose*; raw for draining/abscess indications, stir-fried for Spleen-support and diarrhoea.
- *Contraindicated:* *pregnancy* at medicinal dose (classical *妊娠慎用* plus animal uterine-stimulant data — the convergence noted above); ordinary dietary amounts in pregnancy are a grey zone for a clinician, not a rule.
- *Cautions:* constipation, dehydration, and frank dryness/fluid-deficiency states; theoretical additive hypoglycaemia with antidiabetic medication; *Kanglaite is a pharmaceutical* with its own safety profile, not covered by herbal dosing.
- *Coeliac note:* gluten-free in itself; verify sourcing against "pearl barley" cross-contact.
- *Reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dosing: _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ monograph for 薏苡仁; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (dose range, raw/fried form selection, the "mild and slow" dosing note, pregnancy caution). Reference thresholds requiring current verification, not medical advice.]

